# Task Execution Workflow

Every task (feature, fix, refactor, review, or doc change) follows this sequence. **Do not skip phases** to start coding faster — context gathered here prevents banned approaches, architecture violations, and pattern drift.

**Rule hierarchy:** `docs/requirements.md` → `docs/architecture.md` → `docs/implementation-plan.md` → `.cursor/rules/project-rules.mdc` → `.cursor/rules/senior-standards.mdc`. When the user’s request conflicts with project law, stop and clarify before implementing.

**Single source of truth:** Do not re-implement what already exists. Before creating any new function/module/component/schema/helper, first confirm an equivalent does not already exist. If it exists, **re-use or extend it** rather than creating a parallel implementation.

---

## Phase 1 — Standards and project law (read first)

### 1.1 Senior standards

Read `.cursor/rules/senior-standards.mdc` in full before planning or editing code.

Internalize at minimum:

| Area | Apply during task |
|------|-------------------|
| **No workarounds** | Proper fixes only; no temporary hacks left behind |
| **Existing patterns** | Match structure, naming, and conventions already in the repo |
| **SOLID / DRY / KISS / YAGNI** | Small, focused changes; no speculative scope |
| **Guard clauses** | Early returns; avoid deep nesting |
| **Task completion checklist** | Verify adherence before sign-off; short senior review of what went well / what did not |

### 1.2 Project rules

Read `.cursor/rules/project-rules.mdc` in full. Treat it as **DupBuster-specific law**, not optional context.

Before writing code, you should be able to answer:

- **Milestone scope** — Which milestone (M1–M4) does this task belong to? Do not pull later-milestone work (e.g. M4 redaction CI into M1) unless explicitly tasked.
- **Product invariants** — On-device only, content-only duplicates, review-before-delete, partial-coverage honesty, frozen EN tokens from `src/tokens/tokens.ts`.
- **Architecture boundaries** — What runs in JS vs native ScanEngine; bridge law (4 Hz progress, no paths/hashes/bytes on bridge).
- **Banned approaches** — Scan §11 before proposing stack, bridge, or UX shortcuts.
- **Locked decisions** — §12 items are not relitigated without a doc update.
- **Testing expectations** — §10: which fixtures, native tests, or AC IDs apply to this change.

If the task touches requirements-level behavior, also read the relevant sections of `docs/requirements.md`, `docs/architecture.md`, and `docs/implementation-plan.md` (authority order in `project-rules.mdc` §1).

### 1.3 Knowledge tracking

Read `.cursor/rules/tracking.mdc`. Plan to update `project-rules.mdc` when you learn something future work must not rediscover (failed approaches, new dependencies, build steps, repeated review violations).

---

## Phase 2 — Codebase context (inspect before extending)

Understand **what exists** and **how it is implemented** so new work extends the project instead of inventing parallel patterns.

### 2.1 Orientation (always)

| Question | Where to look |
|----------|----------------|
| What is the directory layout? | `project-rules.mdc` §8; repo root (`android/`, `ios/`, `src/`, `tests/fixtures/`) |
| What milestone is the repo in? | `docs/implementation-plan.md` §2–3; presence/absence of scaffold (`M1-01` may mean docs-only) |
| What is frozen product/design detail? | `docs/requirements.md`, `docs/architecture.md` |

### 2.2 Targeted inspection (task-dependent)

Search and read code **in the areas the task will touch**, not the whole tree blindly:

1. **Identify touch surfaces** — e.g. native ScanEngine, RN bridge, `src/components/`, controllers, tokens, fixtures.
2. **Find the nearest existing implementation** — same layer (native vs JS), same concern (hash, progress, delete, UI catalog).
3. **Locate the single source of truth** — identify the canonical module/type/API that already owns this responsibility; avoid creating a second “almost-the-same” helper.
4. **Extract conventions** — naming, file placement, error types, test layout, how bridge payloads are shaped.
5. **Note gaps honestly** — if scaffold is missing, follow architecture docs and `project-rules.mdc` layout; do not assume Expo or alternate stacks.

Use exploration appropriate to scope: read key files, grep for symbols/patterns, run focused searches under `android/`, `ios/ScanEngine/`, `src/`, `tests/fixtures/dupbuster/v1/`.

### 2.3 Context checklist (mental model before coding)

Before implementation, confirm:

- [ ] I know which **milestone and AC IDs** (if any) this task satisfies.
- [ ] I know whether work belongs in **JS or native** and will not hash/index/delete on the JS thread for scan paths.
- [ ] I will **not** send paths, hashes, or file bytes over the RN bridge.
- [ ] I will use **`tokens.ts` literals** for user-facing EN copy (no paraphrasing).
- [ ] I have checked **§11 banned approaches** — nothing in my plan repeats a listed failure mode.
- [ ] I know **existing patterns** in the touched directories and will match them.
- [ ] I verified the functionality I’m about to add **does not already exist**; if it does, I will **re-use or extend** the existing implementation.

If any item is unclear, inspect more of the codebase or docs before proceeding.

---

## Phase 3 — Plan, implement, verify

### 3.1 Plan

- Restate the task in one sentence aligned with requirements/architecture.
- List files/modules to add or change; call out bridge, schema, or token impacts.
- Explicitly name what will be **re-used/extended** vs what must be **new**, and why new code is necessary.
- Name **AC IDs** or fixtures you will run or extend (`project-rules.mdc` §15, `implementation-plan.md` §9.2).
- If the plan would violate a ban or locked decision, **stop** and propose an alternative aligned with project law.

### 3.2 Implement

- **Native first** for scan/hash/index/delete when applicable (`project-rules.mdc` §13).
- **Minimal diff** — only what the task requires; match surrounding style.
- **Prefer extension over duplication** — update the canonical module/type as the single source of truth; avoid introducing new helpers that compete with existing ones.
- **No banned shortcuts** — e.g. no JS-side hashing, no second catalog store, no bridge payload expansion.
- When adding dependencies or discovering a dead end, update `project-rules.mdc` per `tracking.mdc` in the same session.

### 3.3 Verify

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| **Tests** | Run tests required by `project-rules.mdc` §10 for the change type (native unit, fixtures, component/a11y, etc.) |
| **Build** | Run `./build.sh` when it exists; otherwise document the closest equivalent checks (lint, native tests) in the response |
| **Standards pass** | Walk `senior-standards.mdc` task completion checklist |
| **Project law pass** | Re-check bridge law, tokens, milestone scope, and bans |
| **Knowledge** | Update `project-rules.mdc` if anything learned should be recorded |

### 3.4 Sign-off

End the task response with what was verified, which AC IDs or fixtures were exercised (if any), and any follow-ups. Per `senior-standards.mdc`, end with: **Mischief managed 🪄**

---

## Phase 4 — When the user changes direction

If the user refines scope mid-task, treat it as guidance on the **same** workflow unless they explicitly cancel:

1. Re-check whether the refinement still fits milestone scope and project law.
2. Re-inspect only the **new** touch surfaces if the refinement adds them.
3. Do not abandon recorded bans or decisions without a doc update.

---

## Quick reference — phase order

```
1. Read senior-standards.mdc + project-rules.mdc (+ tracking.mdc)
2. Read authority docs if behavior/architecture is in scope
3. Inspect codebase in areas the task will touch
4. Plan → implement → test → update project-rules if needed → sign-off
```

**Failure mode to avoid:** Implementing from general RN/mobile habit without Phase 1–2 — this is how banned bridge payloads, wrong milestone scope, and paraphrased tokens get introduced.
